WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.273
On a mixed crop and dairy farm, two plans depend on each other.

2
00:00:04.323 --> 00:00:07.995
What to grow on each field, and what to feed each group of cows.

3
00:00:12.073 --> 00:00:16.978
Europe's Common Agricultural Policy now adds rotation rules on the fields.

4
00:00:17.028 --> 00:00:21.009
And climate policy wants less enteric methane from the cows.

5
00:00:21.059 --> 00:00:24.247
Change the diet, and the crop plan has to follow.

6
00:00:24.647 --> 00:00:33.087
This paper builds one optimization model that solves both plans together, and uses it to ask how a methane policy would actually play out.

7
00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:37.505
The model is a mixed integer linear program.

8
00:00:37.555 --> 00:00:46.335
Binary variables choose a crop for every field in every year, from thirty-three crops, with double cropping allowed, over a ten-year horizon.

9
00:00:46.735 --> 00:00:58.931
Continuous variables set the ration for eleven animal groups, from home-grown crops and fifteen purchased feeds, while meeting requirements for intake, energy, protein, fiber, starch and fat.

10
00:00:59.331 --> 00:01:01.552
Rotation rules become constraints.

11
00:01:01.602 --> 00:01:06.550
The same annual species cannot follow itself without a winter or cover crop.

12
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.287
Alfalfa stays up to three and a half years, then takes a two-year break.

13
00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:13.299
Permanent grassland stays put.

14
00:01:13.349 --> 00:01:17.713
And enteric methane is a linear function of energy and fiber intake.

15
00:01:19.663 --> 00:01:21.851
Step one is for the policymaker.

16
00:01:21.901 --> 00:01:29.717
Weight income against methane, solve again and again, and you trace a Pareto front for each of three Italian farms:

17
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:34.397
a standard farm, a Grana Padano farm, and a Parmigiano Reggiano farm.

18
00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:37.712
The trade-off is non-linear, and very uneven.

19
00:01:37.762 --> 00:01:45.190
At maximum mitigation, the standard farm gives up fifty-eight percent of its income for a nine percent methane cut.

20
00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:52.411
The Parmigiano farm, with its higher milk price, loses only about six percent of income for a seven percent cut.

21
00:01:54.061 --> 00:01:55.941
Step two tests two policies.

22
00:01:55.991 --> 00:02:04.726
A methane tax, even at one hundred seventy-six euros per ton of carbon dioxide equivalent, cuts emissions by less than one percent.

23
00:02:04.776 --> 00:02:12.912
At five hundred seventy-five euros, the cut is still under five percent, and the standard farm's net income turns negative.

24
00:02:13.312 --> 00:02:14.780
A hard cap works better.

25
00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:17.633
Reductions of up to nine percent are feasible.

26
00:02:17.683 --> 00:02:19.879
But the shadow price climbs steeply.

27
00:02:19.929 --> 00:02:28.622
On the standard farm, it goes from four and a half euros per kilogram of methane at a one percent cut, to more than two hundred at nine percent.

28
00:02:30.272 --> 00:02:32.026
What changes on the farm?

29
00:02:32.076 --> 00:02:33.275
Diets get denser:

30
00:02:33.325 --> 00:02:35.842
more protein and starch, less fiber.

31
00:02:35.892 --> 00:02:41.046
Corn silage gives way to ryegrass silage, corn grain, sorghum and alfalfa.

32
00:02:41.096 --> 00:02:45.347
Crop plans diversify, and farms buy more feed from the market.

33
00:02:47.847 --> 00:02:48.579
The limits.

34
00:02:48.629 --> 00:02:53.073
Only enteric methane is counted, not emissions from crops or manure.

35
00:02:53.123 --> 00:02:58.805
Milk yield is held fixed, prices and yields are stable, and feed additives are left out.

36
00:03:01.205 --> 00:03:02.070
The takeaway:

37
00:03:02.120 --> 00:03:04.094
plan crops and diets together.

38
00:03:04.144 --> 00:03:07.621
A cap cuts methane where a realistic tax barely does.

39
00:03:07.671 --> 00:03:10.691
And the cost differs sharply from farm to farm.
